{
  "headline": "Fine-tuning scGPT on the CRISPRi screen it is evaluated on, with scGPT's own published perturbation recipe, does not install a gene-specific regulatory signal in the model. Across the 10 fine-tuned models (five gene-table arms in two cell lines), every one leaves the queried response features inside the shuffled-label band, and the highest any reaches is 0.0378, against the same model's static gene table at 0.2279 in HepG2 and 0.2318 in Jurkat. The reason is visible on the model's own prediction head, before any feature swap: the fine-tuned model reproduces the response shared across knockdowns well (0.6944 in HepG2, 0.4471 in Jurkat) but scores 0.0047 and -0.0176 once the held-out fold's own mean is removed from both sides, the only reading that asks whether it separates one perturbation from another. Fine-tuning teaches the average knockdown response, not which gene was knocked down.",
  "assessment": "The pre-registered prediction is confirmed in its first half and overtaken in its second. It said the response features would stay in the null band while the model might still predict better; the features do stay in the band, but the model does not predict the perturbation-specific part better either, so the null is a property of the fine-tuned model rather than of the read-out procedure. That is the strongest form the result could take, because it does not depend on the translator, the query family, or the feature standardisation: the same conclusion is reached by scoring the model's own output directly.\n\nScope and limits. This is one architecture and one recipe, run on the screen the question is about, which is the setting most favourable to installing the signal and therefore the setting in which a null is most informative; it does not speak to other architectures or to fine-tuning on a different screen. A fine-tuned model may only be scored on perturbations it never saw, so the main arms read two of run 3's five folds. That costs resolution and the report states it: in HepG2 the shuffled-label draws reach 0.0550 and co-expression itself scores 0.0072 on the 54 response groups these folds leave; in Jurkat the shuffled-label draws reach 0.0555 and co-expression itself scores 0.0259 on the 137 response groups these folds leave. The claim the design supports is therefore that fine-tuning does not lift the response features anywhere near the gene table and does not move them detectably at all, not that they are exactly zero. Three controls address the obvious objections: the arm is refitted on the complementary folds so the pooled result covers the whole screen, the batch sampler is run in the exact upstream configuration as well as ours, and because the recipe's selection rule picks epoch 1 in Jurkat the features are also read off the last epoch, which the rule rejected.",
  "decision_rule": {
    "preregistered": "run 3's rule, unchanged: residual target, reliability-weighted, ceiling-corrected per-perturbation Pearson correlation, median over held-out perturbations of the grouped split, read against the shuffled-label band and against the same model's static gene-table arm",
    "restriction_added_by_this_run": "only folds 0 and 1 are scored, because a fine-tuned model must not be scored on a perturbation it trained on. The validation split used for epoch selection is drawn from the TRAINING folds, never from these",
    "reported_beside_it": "the fold-centred version of the same correlation, and the model's own predicted response scored with no translator in the path",
    "not_substituted": "the verdict is read from the pre-registered cell. The direct-prediction result is additional evidence about the model, not a replacement metric"
  },
  "methods": {
    "base_model": "wanglab/scGPT-human at a24c237737a40f3720f75abb555489e9fe753be6",
    "recipe": "scGPT's published perturbation tutorial: masked_mse_loss(all positions), Adam at lr 0.0001, batch 64, up to 15 epochs with early stop after 10, schedule gamma 0.9; selection on masked MSE on held-out validation cells (the tutorial's rule)",
    "arms": {
      "own_trainable": "the standard published recipe",
      "own_frozen": "gain attributable to the readout alone",
      "esm2_trainable": "protein provenance as the starting table",
      "esm2_frozen": "the same, with the table held fixed",
      "rand_frozen": "capacity-matched null for the two ESM2 arms: the same width and the same learned projection, over vectors that carry no information about the gene"
    },
    "heldout_folds": [
      0,
      1
    ],
    "feature_queries": "run 2's value-substitution family, unmodified, so the fine-tuned numbers are commensurable with the frozen ones; plus the model's own perturbation-flag query, which is the query the fine-tuning objective is defined on",
    "restatement_checks": "the masked-MSE loss is compared numerically against the installed scgpt/loss.py at load time and the result recorded in each run's provenance.json; --upstream-sampling reproduces the published batch sampler exactly, including the ~40% of rows that lose their perturbed gene",
    "n_training_runs": 16
  },
